<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meñli I Giray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1445">1445</a>–<a href="/years/1515">1515</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Mengli I Giray, Mengli Giray · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giray-dynasty">Giray dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meñli I Giray was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, with interruptions, from 1468 to 1515. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took the Crimean coast in 1475 he accepted Ottoman overlordship, and in 1502 he destroyed the Great Horde, the last remnant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Allied with Muscovy, he raided Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zincirli-medrese">Zincirli Medrese</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchysarai</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menli-i-giray.jpg" alt="Meñli I Giray" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Turkish miniature (XVI century) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Me%C3%B1li_I_Giray.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Meñli I Giray was the sixth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haci-i-giray">Hacı I Giray</a>. When Hacı Giray died, power passed to his eldest son Nur Devlet, who was supported by the Great Horde, and Meñli revolted with the backing of the Crimean nobility. In 1467 he occupied the capital, Kyrk-Er, but Nur Devlet soon drove him out and he fled to the Genoese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>.</p><p>In June 1468 a delegation of nobles elected him khan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>. He marched on the capital with the nobles and a Genoese detachment, and after six months Nur Devlet was expelled; he fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> but was captured and imprisoned in the Genoese fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudak">Sudak</a>. Meñli was khan three times in all, and it took him twelve years to establish himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In his second reign Meñli made an anti-Turkish alliance with the Principality of Theodoro, and in the summer of 1469 a Turkish fleet burned villages near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>. In March 1475 the nobles replaced him with his elder brother Hayder, and in May a large Turkish fleet took Kaffa, other Genoese forts and Theodoro. Meñli, who had supported the Genoese, was captured and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In the spring of 1478 he was released and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> with a Turkish fleet and troops, and became khan as a Turkish vassal.</p><p>In 1480 he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>, the grand prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, against Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, the Great Horde and the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>, an alliance that counted in the great stand on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugra-river">Ugra River</a>. In September 1482 he ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and took Ivan Chodkiewicz and his family hostage, and from 1489 to 1500 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> repeatedly devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a> and Volyn. In 1502 he defeated the last khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, took its capital, Saray, and proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a>. Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> formed a significant part of his army, and he founded the fortress of Özü.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Meñli was buried in the dürbe of Salaçıq in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchysarai</a>. In that city he commissioned the Zıncırlı Medrese in 1500, the dürbe in 1501 and the Demir Qapı, or Iron Gate, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai-palace">Bakhchysarai Palace</a> in 1503, the last by Aloisio the New. Two of his sons, Mehmed I Giray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahib-i-giray">Sahib I Giray</a>, also became khans. His daughter Ayşe Hatun married first Şehzade Mehmed, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Sultan Bayezid II</a>, and after him his half-brother Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>. One of his wives, Nur Sultan, was one of only three women known to have had a political role in the Crimean Khanate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meñli I Giray” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1475 an Ottoman fleet under Gedik Ahmed Pasha conquered Caffa and Theodoro, and <b>Mengli I Giray</b>, restored to the throne with Ottoman support in 1478, accepted the suzerainty of the sultan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1502 <b>Meñli I Giray</b> of Crimea defeated Sheikh Ahmed, the last khan of the Great Horde, and sacked Sarai.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1502 the Crimean khan <b>Meñli I Giray</b> sacked Sarai and destroyed the horde.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-horde">Great Horde</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchysarai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45,34&amp;year=1490" aria-label="Crimea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5359 574 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Meñli I Giray"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5809 844h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5809 844h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45,34&amp;year=1490">What was here in 1490? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
